<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jovan Dragaš</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1378</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jovan Dejanović</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jovan Dragaš was a Serbian nobleman, son of the sebastokrator Dejan and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>. After Dušan's death in 1355 he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas">Konstantin Dragaš</a> built an autonomous lordship in north-eastern Macedonia, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyustendil">Kyustendil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strumica">Strumica</a>. After the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> in 1371 the brothers became Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jovan-dragas.jpg" alt="Jovan Dragaš" width="320" height="550" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown_nobleman,_Belu%C4%87a_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
